\title{Gated Readouts and Coprime Boundary Blocks:\\ A Two-Layer Theory of Internalization}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{15 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a two-layer arithmetic theory of \emph{boundary-block internalization}:
whether an answer-producing process, having learned to use a distinguished marker
occupying $k$ exclusive dimensions, still requires that marker at evaluation time.
The first layer is an \emph{additive gate}: a threshold $\thr$ compared against the
aggregate drive $\sum_{i=1}^{k} w_i$. We compute the exact arithmetic of four local
interventions (whole-block zeroing, single-coordinate zeroing, single-coordinate sign
flip, rescaling) and derive four structural laws. On a uniform block of $k$
coordinates of size $a$, single-coordinate zeroing survives iff $\thr \le (k-1)a$ and
a sign flip survives iff $\thr\le(k-2)a$; the vanishing of $k-2$ at $k=2$ makes sign
sensitivity an \emph{exact} dependence test at width two and uninformative at every
larger width. Modelling a learner by width-independent parameters $(b,g,d,\sepp)$,
boundary dependence is the width-free condition $b<d$ and hence width-invariant, while
curing, $\sepp\le k \wedge d\le b+kg$, is monotone in $k$: width sets the probability
of a cure, the learner sets internalization. Retention $\rho(k)=b/(b+kg)$ is strictly
decreasing with $\rho(k)\to0$ and $k\rho(k)\to b/g$, so dependence deepens only
harmonically and $\sum_k\rho(k)$ diverges. The width-one outcome and the trait are
logically independent, which forces the separation parameter into the model. The
second layer is arithmetic: modelling $k$ exclusive dimensions by pairwise coprime
moduli $m_i\ge2$, the Chinese Remainder Theorem becomes an ablation statement --- a
block resolves every range below its capacity $\prod_i m_i \ge 2^k$, within the margin
$A\le2^{k-1}$ every single-dimension ablation is a no-op, and the empty block resolves
only ranges with at most one element --- realised at every width by Fermat blocks.
Two honest boundaries delimit the theory: the single-drop no-op needs the margin
($(2,3,5)$ resolves $30$ but loses resolution when $5$ is dropped), and sign flips are
always free at the capacity layer, so sign sensitivity cannot be a capacity phenomenon.
We conclude with identifiability results and falsifiable predictions.
\end{abstract}

