\section{Introduction}

Consider a process that must emit an answer and which, during training, has available a
distinguished \emph{boundary marker} occupying $k$ dedicated coordinates of its state.
Two questions are separable and routinely conflated: does the process learn the task at
all (\emph{curing}), and, once it has, does it still need the marker at evaluation time
(the negation of which we call \emph{internalization})?

A campaign of controlled interventions distinguishes them. Hold the learner fixed, vary
only the width $k$, and intervene at evaluation time only, in four ways: $\zeroN$ (zero
the whole block), $\zeroone_j$ (zero one coordinate), $\flipone_j$ (negate one
coordinate), and $\mathrm{scale}_c$ (multiply the block by $c$). Five regularities
emerge and are the target of this paper.

\begin{enumerate}
\item[(O1)] \textbf{Collective use.} $\zeroone$ is a no-op at every width $k\ge2$, in
every arm.
\item[(O2)] \textbf{A fixed dependent set.} $\zeroN$ costs a substantial fraction of
accuracy on a fixed subset of learners and nothing on the rest --- and the same subset
is dependent at $k=2$ and $k=3$.
\item[(O3)] \textbf{Dependence deepens.} On dependent learners the cost of $\zeroN$
grows with $k$.
\item[(O4)] \textbf{Sign sensitivity is width-two only.} $\flipone$ costs a substantial
fraction in dependent arms at $k=2$ and is exactly free in all arms at $k=3$.
\item[(O5)] \textbf{No width-one predictor.} The $k=1$ behaviour of a learner does not
determine whether it will be boundary-dependent at the widths where it cures.
\end{enumerate}

We show these are theorems of a two-parameter gate model, supplemented by an arithmetic
model of what a block of exclusive dimensions can carry. The \emph{gate layer}
(Sections~\ref{sec:gate}--\ref{sec:k1}) answers \emph{when does damage cross a
threshold}; the \emph{capacity layer} (Section~\ref{sec:crt}) answers \emph{why can $k$
exclusive dimensions carry an answer}. Each layer explicitly excludes itself from part
of the other's territory.

\section{The gated readout}\label{sec:gate}

Drives are rational; every statement holds verbatim over $\mathbb{R}$.

\begin{definition}
A \emph{gated readout of width $k$} is a pair $R=(\thr,\mathrm{coord})$ with
$\thr\in\mathbb{Q}$ and $\mathrm{coord}\colon\{1,\dots,k\}\to\mathbb{Q}$. For
$w\colon\{1,\dots,k\}\to\mathbb{Q}$ set $\dr(w)=\sum_{i=1}^k w_i$. The readout
\emph{survives} $w$ when $\thr\le\dr(w)$.
\end{definition}

The single modelling commitment is that the block is read as an aggregate. Observation
(O1) is the empirical warrant; Section~\ref{sec:crt} supplies an independent arithmetic
one.

\begin{definition}
$\zeroN(w)\equiv0$; $\zeroone_j(w)$ agrees with $w$ off $j$ and is $0$ at $j$;
$\flipone_j(w)$ agrees with $w$ off $j$ and is $-w_j$ at $j$;
$\mathrm{scale}_c(w)_i=c\,w_i$. The readout is \emph{boundary-dependent} if it does not
survive $\zeroN(\mathrm{coord})$, and \emph{self-sufficient} if it does.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact intervention arithmetic]\label{lem:arith}
For all $w$ and $j$,
\[
\dr(\zeroN(w))=0,\quad \dr(\zeroone_j(w))=\dr(w)-w_j,\quad
\dr(\flipone_j(w))=\dr(w)-2w_j,\quad \dr(\mathrm{scale}_c(w))=c\,\dr(w).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Split the sum as $w_j+\sum_{i\ne j}w_i$; the intervention alters only the isolated
term, replacing $w_j$ by $0$ or $-w_j$; the rescaling factors out by distributivity.
\end{proof}

The asymmetry between deletion (cost $w_j$) and flipping (cost $2w_j$) is the origin of
(O4).

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:dep}
$R$ is boundary-dependent iff $\thr>0$, and self-sufficient iff $\thr\le0$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\dr(\zeroN(w))=0$, so survival of $\zeroN$ reads $\thr\le0$.
\end{proof}

The criterion mentions neither $k$ nor the block; everything in Section~\ref{sec:seeds}
is a corollary of that absence.

\begin{proposition}
$R$ fails to survive $\mathrm{scale}_c(w)$ iff $c\,\dr(w)<\thr$. If $c\ge0$ and
$\dr(w)\ge0$, a self-sufficient readout survives every rescaling.
\end{proposition}

This is the qualitative account of a $\mathrm{scale}_{0.1}$ arm: a mild graded cost on
dependent arms, exactly zero on self-sufficient ones.

\section{Uniform blocks: the $k-1$ and $k-2$ laws}

Write $\unif(k,a)$ for the block with every coordinate equal to $a$.

\begin{lemma}
$\dr(\unif(k,a))=ka$, $\dr(\zeroone_j\unif(k,a))=(k-1)a$, and
$\dr(\flipone_j\unif(k,a))=(k-2)a$. Consequently $\zeroone_j$ is survived iff
$\thr\le(k-1)a$ and $\flipone_j$ is survived iff $\thr\le(k-2)a$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Collective use, gate version --- (O1)]\label{thm:collgate}
Let $a>0$, $k\ge2$ and $\thr\le(k-1)a$. Then for every $j$ the readout survives
$\zeroone_j\unif(k,a)$ and survives the intact block --- whether or not it is
boundary-dependent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The first claim is the survival law; for the second, $(k-1)a\le ka$ and survival is
monotone in the drive.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sign sensitivity at width two]\label{thm:flip2}
A boundary-dependent width-two readout survives no single sign flip, for any $a$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Survival requires $\thr\le(2-2)a=0$, contradicting $\thr>0$
(Proposition~\ref{prop:dep}).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Flip is an exact dependence marker at width two]\label{thm:marker}
For a width-two readout, any $a$ and any $j$: the readout survives
$\flipone_j\unif(2,a)$ if and only if it is self-sufficient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides are the condition $\thr\le0$.
\end{proof}

At $k=2$ a flip forces sign opposition between the two coordinates and the aggregate
collapses to exactly the value $\zeroN$ produces; the flip reading is therefore not a
heuristic for dependence but equivalent to it, with no error in either direction.

\begin{theorem}[Flip freedom at width three]\label{thm:flip3}
A width-three readout with $\thr\le a$ survives every single sign flip --- even if it is
boundary-dependent.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Survival requires $\thr\le(3-2)a=a$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The marker is width-two only]
There are a width-two readout, a width-three readout and $a>0$ such that both are
boundary-dependent, both survive their intact blocks, no flip is survived at width two,
and every flip is survived at width three.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $a=1$ and $\thr=1$ in both cases with uniform blocks of size $1$: dependence is
$1>0$, intact survival is $1\le k$, and the flip claims are
Theorems~\ref{thm:flip2} and~\ref{thm:flip3}.
\end{proof}

Thus the contrast between a large flip effect at $k=2$ and exactly none at $k=3$ needs
no change of mechanism: it is the single law $\thr\le(k-2)a$ evaluated at the root of
its own coefficient.

\begin{theorem}[Severity staircase]\label{thm:stair}
For $a\ge0$, $k\ge2$ and any $j$: self-sufficiency $\Rightarrow$ survives $\flipone_j$
$\Rightarrow$ survives $\zeroone_j$ $\Rightarrow$ survives the control.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Chain $0\le(k-2)a\le(k-1)a\le ka$ against the monotone survival criterion.
\end{proof}

An arm in which a flip hits but whole-block zeroing does not is therefore impossible
under the model.

\section{Seeds: width sets curing, the learner sets internalization}\label{sec:seeds}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{seed} is a tuple $s=(b,g,d,\sepp)$ with $b,g,d\in\mathbb{Q}$, $g\ge0$ and
$\sepp\in\mathbb{N}$: the \emph{base} drive produced internally, the \emph{gain}
contributed by each exclusive boundary dimension, the \emph{demand}, and the number of
exclusive dimensions needed to resolve the boundary at all. At width $k$ the seed
trains into the readout $R_k=(d-b,\ \unif(k,g))$, and \emph{cures at width $k$} iff
\[
\sepp \le k \quad\text{(resolution)}\qquad\text{and}\qquad d\le b+kg \quad\text{(capacity)}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Curing is width-monotone]\label{thm:mono}
If $s$ cures at $k$ and $k\le m$, then $s$ cures at $m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\sepp\le k\le m$, and $kg\le mg$ since $g\ge0$, so $d\le b+kg\le b+mg$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Internalization is width-invariant: the headline law]\label{thm:head}
For every seed and all widths $k,m$, $R_k$ is boundary-dependent iff $R_m$ is.
Moreover $R_k$ is boundary-dependent iff $b<d$, and self-sufficient iff $d\le b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:dep}, dependence of $R_k$ is $d-b>0$, in which $k$ does not
occur.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The dependent set is width-invariant --- (O2)]\label{cor:set}
For any family $(s_i)_{i\in I}$ of seeds and any widths $k,m$,
\[
\{\,i : R_k(s_i)\text{ boundary-dependent}\,\}=\{\,i : R_m(s_i)\text{ boundary-dependent}\,\}.
\]
\end{corollary}

Corollary~\ref{cor:set} formalises the slogan \emph{width sets the probability of a
cure; the learner sets internalization}. It also disciplines a reporting error: if one
sample of learners shows a high internalization rate and another a lower one, the trait
cannot have changed with the width, and the honest summary is the pooled rate over
learners.

\subsection{Retention}

\begin{definition}
The \emph{retention} of a seed at width $k$ is $\rho_s(k)=b/(b+kg)$: the share of the
required drive surviving whole-block ablation.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:ret}
If $b\ne0$ and $g=0$ then $\rho_s\equiv1$. If $b>0$ and $g>0$ then $\rho_s$ is strictly
decreasing, $\rho_s(k)<1$ for $k\ge1$, and $\rho_s(k)\to0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Denominators are positive; for $k<m$, clearing denominators in
$\rho_s(k)>\rho_s(m)$ reduces to $b\,g\,(m-k)>0$. For the limit, $b+kg\to\infty$ with
constant numerator.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Harmonic law and divergence]\label{thm:harm}
If $b,g>0$ then $k\rho_s(k)\to b/g$, and $\sum_k \rho_s(k)$ diverges.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$k\rho_s(k)=(b/g)(1-\rho_s(k))$ is an identity, and $\rho_s(k)\to0$. For divergence,
$\rho_s(k)\ge \frac{b}{b+g}\cdot\frac{1}{k+1}$ for all $k\ge0$, and the harmonic series
diverges.
\end{proof}

A mechanism with geometric collapse of retention, $\rho(k)\asymp2^{-k}$, is therefore
incompatible with an additive per-dimension gain: Theorem~\ref{thm:harm} is a
prohibition, not merely a rate.

\section{Why there is no width-one predictor}\label{sec:k1}

Under a capacity-only model --- curing meaning only $d\le b+kg$ --- a self-sufficient
seed has $d\le b\le b+g$ and would necessarily cure at $k=1$, so ``failed at $k=1$''
would perfectly predict dependence. The observed absence of a predictor is thus
evidence for the resolution parameter.

\begin{theorem}[Logical independence]\label{thm:indep}
For each pair of truth values $(\beta_1,\beta_2)$ there is a seed that cures at width
$1$ iff $\beta_1$, is boundary-dependent at width $2$ iff $\beta_2$, and cures at width
$2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $(b,g,d,\sepp)$ equal to $(2,1,1,2)$, $(1,1,3,1)$, $(2,1,1,1)$, $(1,1,2,1)$
respectively; each claim is a numerical check of $\sepp\le k$, $d\le b+kg$ and $b<d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[An indistinguishable pair with opposite traits]\label{thm:pair}
There are seeds $s,t$ with: neither cures at width $1$, both cure at width $2$, $s$ is
boundary-dependent at every width, and $t$ is self-sufficient at every width.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$s=(1,1,3,1)$ fails at $k=1$ for lack of capacity ($1+1<3$); $t=(2,1,1,2)$ fails for
lack of resolution ($2>1$); both cure at $k=2$; and $b<d$ for $s$ while $d\le b$ for
$t$, at every width by Theorem~\ref{thm:head}.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:head} and~\ref{thm:pair} show that (O2) and (O5) are not in tension:
the trait is constant across widths and invisible at the lowest rung, because resolution
failure is trait-neutral. Operationally, internalization must be measured at a width
where the learner cures.

\section{The capacity layer: exclusive dimensions and the Chinese Remainder Theorem}\label{sec:crt}

Exclusivity is modelled as pairwise coprimality: distinct dimensions share no
information.

\begin{definition}
Let $S$ be a finite set of surviving dimensions with moduli $m\colon S\to\mathbb{Z}$.
Say $S$ \emph{resolves the answer range} $A\in\mathbb{Z}$ if for all integers
$0\le x,y<A$, the condition $m_i\mid x-y$ for all $i\in S$ implies $x=y$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Capacity: CRT as an ablation statement]\label{thm:cap}
If the moduli on $S$ are pairwise coprime and $A\le\prod_{i\in S}m_i$, then $S$ resolves
$A$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Pairwise coprimality lifts $m_i\mid x-y$ for all $i$ to $\prod_i m_i\mid x-y$; but
$|x-y|<A\le\prod_i m_i$, and the only such multiple is $0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Width is capacity]\label{lem:width}
If $m_i\ge2$ for all $i\in S$ and $|S|=k$, then $\prod_{i\in S}m_i\ge2^k$; hence
$\prod_{i\in S\setminus\{j\}}m_i\ge2^{k-1}$ for any $j\in S$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Single-drop no-op; whole-block ablation is fatal]\label{thm:drop}
Let $|S|=k$ with pairwise coprime moduli $m_i\ge2$ and $A\le2^{k-1}$. Then
$S\setminus\{j\}$ resolves $A$ for every $j\in S$. Moreover the empty block resolves
$A$ if and only if $A\le1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Coprimality is inherited by subsets, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:width} the surviving capacity
is at least $2^{k-1}\ge A$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}. For the empty block: if
$A\le1$ the range has at most one element, and if $A\ge2$ then $x=0$, $y=1$ satisfy the
vacuous hypothesis and differ.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Collective use, capacity version --- (O1) again]\label{thm:collcap}
Let $|S|=k$ with pairwise coprime moduli $m_i\ge2$ and $2\le A\le2^{k-1}$ (possible
exactly when $k\ge2$). Then $S$ resolves $A$; every $S\setminus\{j\}$ resolves $A$; and
the empty block does not.
\end{theorem}

Collective use thus holds twice over, for two different reasons --- an additive margin at
the gate (Theorem~\ref{thm:collgate}) and an arithmetic margin here --- which is why (O1)
is robust and weak evidence for either layer individually.

\begin{theorem}[Realisation at every width: Fermat blocks]\label{thm:fermat}
For every $k\ge2$ there is a $k$-element block with pairwise coprime moduli, all at
least $2$, and a range $A\ge2$ satisfying all conclusions of Theorem~\ref{thm:collcap}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $m_i=F_i=2^{2^i}+1$ for $i<k$, i.e. $3,5,17,257,65537,\dots$; each exceeds $2$, and
distinct Fermat numbers are coprime by the telescoping identity
$F_0F_1\cdots F_{n-1}=F_n-2$ together with oddness. Take $A=2^{k-1}\ge2$.
\end{proof}

An ideal $k$-dimensional exclusive block carries $\log_2\prod_i m_i\ge k$ bits, of which
the design uses at most $k-1$, spending one bit's worth of capacity on single-point
redundancy.

\subsection{Two honest boundaries}

\begin{theorem}[The single-drop no-op requires the margin]\label{thm:margin}
There are a pairwise coprime block, an index $j$ and a range $A$ such that the block
resolves $A$ but the ablated block does not.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Moduli $(2,3,5)$ with $A=30$: resolution holds by Theorem~\ref{thm:cap}, but dropping
the modulus $5$ leaves $0$ and $6$ congruent modulo $2$ and $3$ and distinct in
$[0,30)$.
\end{proof}

Hence a rule of the form ``at least three exclusive dimensions and you are safe'' is
correct only relative to the answer range: the free deletion is bought by the margin
$A\le2^{k-1}$, and a block run at capacity has no redundancy at all.

\begin{theorem}[Sign flips are free at the capacity layer]\label{thm:signfree}
Replacing $m_i$ by $-m_i$ on any subset of dimensions leaves the resolved ranges
unchanged.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$m\mid n$ iff $-m\mid n$, and resolution is defined purely by divisibility.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:signfree} localises (O4) unambiguously in the additive gate: sign
sensitivity cannot be a capacity phenomenon. The two layers do not compete for the same
observations.

\section{Identifiability}

\begin{theorem}[The zero battery identifies the block]
If $\dr(w)=\dr(v)$ and $\dr(\zeroone_j w)=\dr(\zeroone_j v)$ for every $j$, then $w=v$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:arith}, $\dr(w)-w_j=\dr(v)-v_j$; subtract the control equality.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Flip readings are redundant at the block level]
$\dr(\flipone_j w)=2\,\dr(\zeroone_j w)-\dr(w)$ for all $w,j$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both sides equal $\dr(w)-2w_j$.
\end{proof}

The control plus the $k$ zeroings is a complete battery for the block; flip arms add
nothing about the block, and carry information only through the gate --- which is exactly
why their informativeness is width-dependent while the zero battery's is not.

\begin{theorem}[One reading determines the retention profile]\label{thm:profile}
Let $s,t$ be seeds with $b_s,b_t>0$. If $\rho_s(k)=\rho_t(k)$ for a single $k\ge1$, then
$\rho_s(m)=\rho_t(m)$ for every $m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Clearing denominators and cancelling $k>0$ gives $b_s g_t=b_t g_s$, i.e. equal ratios
$g/b$; and $\rho(m)=1/(1+m\,(g/b))$ depends on the seed only through that ratio.
\end{proof}

Two learners agreeing on retention at one width and disagreeing at another would falsify
the additive gate. Positively, one retention reading plus the smallest curing width
calibrates all four seed parameters up to scale, after which every width is predicted
with no free parameters.

\section{A recorded table, checked exactly}

Consider a campaign covering twelve learners indexed $8$--$19$, trained at widths $2$
and $3$ with everything but the width held fixed, and evaluated after whole-block
ablation. Control-normalised retentions, as exact rationals:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
learner & $k=2$ & $k=3$\\
\midrule
13 & $0.7544$ & $0.7041$\\
14 & $0.9141$ & $0.9014$\\
15 & $0.8037$ & $0.7104$\\
17 & $0.9067$ & $0.7437$\\
all others & $1$ (within noise) & $1$ (within noise)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

With a dependence cut at retention $\le0.95$, three finite decidable claims hold
exactly: (i) for every learner, retention at $k=2$ is below the cut iff retention at
$k=3$ is (an instance of Corollary~\ref{cor:set}); (ii) that common set is exactly
$\{13,14,15,17\}$, so eight of twelve learners read as self-sufficient at both widths;
(iii) for every dependent learner, retention at $k=3$ is strictly below retention at
$k=2$ (an instance of Theorem~\ref{thm:ret}).

Claim (ii) carries a methodological warning: if one sub-family of six learners shows one
dependent and another shows three, the correct summary is the pooled rate over all
twelve, since by Theorem~\ref{thm:head} the trait cannot have changed with the width.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What is explained, and by which layer}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Observation & Layer & Statement\\
\midrule
(O1) single ablation free & both & Theorems~\ref{thm:collgate}, \ref{thm:collcap}\\
(O2) same dependent set & gate & Theorem~\ref{thm:head}, Corollary~\ref{cor:set}\\
(O3) dependence deepens & gate & Theorems~\ref{thm:ret}, \ref{thm:harm}\\
(O4) sign sensitivity at $k=2$ only & gate only & Theorems~\ref{thm:flip2}--\ref{thm:flip3}; excluded from capacity by~\ref{thm:signfree}\\
(O5) no width-one predictor & gate, two-parameter & Theorems~\ref{thm:indep}, \ref{thm:pair}\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Scope and idealisations}

\emph{Uniformity.} For non-uniform blocks, $(k-1)a$ becomes $\dr(w)-w_j$ and $(k-2)a$
becomes $\dr(w)-2w_j$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:arith}); the qualitative laws persist, only the
clean numerology is uniform-block specific. In particular a dominant coordinate,
$w_j>\tfrac12\dr(w)$, makes a flip fatal at any width, so ``no flip effect at $k=3$'' is
also weak evidence that trained blocks are roughly balanced.

\emph{Linearity} of the gate is the minimal hypothesis that makes the battery
analysable, warranted by (O1) and the graded response to rescaling. \emph{Additivity}
of gain underlies $\rho(k)=b/(b+kg)$ and is falsifiable by
Theorem~\ref{thm:harm}. \emph{Separation} as an integer threshold is crude; the capacity
layer suggests $\sepp\approx\lceil\log_2 A\rceil$ (or one more if single-point
redundancy is demanded), which couples the two layers quantitatively.

\subsection{Falsifiable predictions}

(P1) no flip effect at any width $k\ge3$ for balanced blocks; (P2) at $k=2$, flip and
whole-block readings agree arm by arm; (P3) the dependent set is identical at every
curing width; (P4) retention profiles never cross; (P5) retention decays harmonically,
never geometrically; (P6) no staircase violations (Theorem~\ref{thm:stair}); (P7) the
first curing width tracks $\log_2$ of the answer-range size.

\subsection{Design consequences}

Width buys success, not independence: increasing $k$ monotonically improves the chance of
curing (Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}) and strictly worsens the retention of a dependent learner
(Theorem~\ref{thm:ret}). Redundancy has an explicit budget, $\thr\le(k-1)a$ at the gate
and $A\le2^{k-1}$ at the capacity layer, and fails outside it
(Theorem~\ref{thm:margin}). Because the trait is invisible below the curing width, the
only sound procedure is to run the whole-block ablation at a width where the learner is
already correct, and to keep re-serving the boundary marker for learners that read as
dependent.

\section{Future work}

A two-parameter rigidity programme: for every learner there should exist
width-independent $(b,g,d)$ and integer $\sepp$ with curing iff $\sepp\le k$ and
$d\le b+kg$, and dependence at every curing width iff $b<d$; the two apparently
paradoxical observations are then jointly forced. Extending the width sweep to $k=4,5$
tests the sign-sensitivity prediction directly. Coupling the layers --- replacing the
integer $\sepp$ by the capacity condition $A\le\prod_i m_i$ --- yields a single model in
which resolution and drive both derive from the block. Finally, whether the observed
deepening is better fitted by a gain growing with $k$ is decidable by the harmonic law:
faster-than-harmonic decay would refute pure additivity.

\section{Conclusion}

A single comparison $b<d$, in which the width does not appear, explains why dependence
on a boundary marker is a trait of the learner rather than of the marker's width; a
monotone pair of conditions explains why width nonetheless governs success; the gap
between the exponents $k-1$ and $k-2$ explains why sign sensitivity is an accident of
width two; the Chinese Remainder Theorem explains why a block of exclusive dimensions can
carry an answer at all and why single-point damage to it is free; and two negative
results --- sign flips free at the capacity layer, redundancy failing outside an explicit
margin --- keep the two layers honest about their jurisdictions.

